Hmm, it doesn't really match what I remember reading about this battle, so I did a quick search on Wikipedia:
“Some confusion reigned among the German ships as to the fate of the two battleship cruisers, which had disappeared into the dark after ceasing fire, and a chase began.
Leipzig saw something on fire, but as it approached it saw that it was just a wreck.
The Nürnberg, slower than the other German ships, joined the battle late and saw the Monmouth, leaning and severely damaged but still moving.
After pointing his spotlight on the ship's flag, the proposal to surrender was refused and he opened the fire, finally flowing the ship.
Without reliable information, von Spee judged that the Good Hope had to escape and stopped the search at 10:15 p.m.
Worried by reports of a British battleship in the vicinity, he set sail to the north.”
So, if this is true, the Germans, groping in the darkness before the invention of the radar, did not know that a ship had sunkewn, and therefore could not look for survivors.
They also thought that there was a larger Royal Navy battleship in the area that could have damaged them badly, so in confusion they fled to take advantage of their advance.
